--TEST--
`Core\Test::assertCount` holds exactly where `Core\Arr::count` answers the expected number — a length-by-expectation grid, both neighbours of every diagonal refused, and `uint`'s far end named in the report — `rule:testing/assertions-are-typed`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `assertCount`'s subject is an `array<T>` and its expectation a `uint`, which
// is `Core\Arr::count`'s own signature -- `nvs_stdlib::test`'s doc comment owns
// why, and the consequence worth pinning is that the two members must never be
// able to disagree. This asks the whole grid rather than a line of it: every
// length against every expectation, counted, so a member that stopped one entry
// early still prints plausibly on its own row and fails here.

use Core\Test;

final class Ask {
    public static function counted(array<string> $subject, uint $expected): string {
        try {
            Test::assertCount($subject, $expected);
        } catch (Core\Test\Failure $fromCount) {
            return "failed";
        }
        return "held";
    }

    public static function nested(array<array<string>> $subject, uint $expected): string {
        try {
            Test::assertCount($subject, $expected);
        } catch (Core\Test\Failure $fromNested) {
            return "failed";
        }
        return "held";
    }

    public static function reported(array<string> $subject, uint $expected): string {
        try {
            Test::assertCount($subject, $expected);
        } catch (Core\Test\Failure $fromReport) {
            return $fromReport->message;
        }
        return "held";
    }
}

// 1. The grid: five subjects of length 0 through 4, each asked about every
// expectation from 0 through 5. `held` must fall on the diagonal and nowhere
// else, which is the bound asserted on both sides six times over -- one entry
// short and one entry long are both present for every length.
array<string> $rows = [];
int $onDiagonal = 0;
int $offDiagonal = 0;
int $agreed = 0;

int $length = 0;
for (; $length < 5; $length = $length + 1) {
    int $expected = 0;
    for (; $expected < 6; $expected = $expected + 1) {
        string $verdict = Ask::counted($rows, $expected as uint);
        if (Core\Arr::count($rows) == $expected) {
            if ($verdict == "held") {
                $onDiagonal = $onDiagonal + 1;
                $agreed = $agreed + 1;
            }
        } else {
            if ($verdict == "failed") {
                $offDiagonal = $offDiagonal + 1;
                $agreed = $agreed + 1;
            }
        }
    }
    $rows[] = "row";
}

echo "onDiagonal=", $onDiagonal, "/5 offDiagonal=", $offDiagonal, "/25 agreed=", $agreed, "/30\n";

// 2. The low end is a real row, not an absence: an empty array holds against
// `0` and fails against `1`, and the loop above has already asked it.
array<string> $empty = [];
echo Ask::counted($empty, 0), " ", Ask::counted($empty, 1), "\n";

// 3. What a count is *not*: it is entries, not distinct values, not keys of a
// particular shape, and not anything below the top level. All three subjects
// answer the number of entries `Core\Arr::count` answers and nothing else.
array<string> $dupes = ["x", "x", "x"];
array<string> $map = ["a" => "1", "b" => "2"];
array<array<string>> $deep = [["a", "b", "c"], ["d"]];

echo Ask::counted($dupes, 3), " ", Ask::counted($map, 2), " ", Ask::nested($deep, 2), "\n";
echo Ask::nested($deep, 4), "\n";

// 4. The count follows the subject rather than a length taken once: removing an
// entry moves the expectation that holds, and re-adding one moves it back.
unset($map["a"]);
echo Ask::counted($map, 1), " ", Ask::counted($map, 2), "\n";
$map["c"] = "3";
echo Ask::counted($map, 2), "\n";

// 5. `uint`'s far end. An expectation no array can ever reach is an ordinary
// failure with both sides named -- not a refusal, and not a wrap back into
// range. The literal is past `int` and is legal only because the parameter is a
// `uint` (`rule:types/arithmetic`).
echo Ask::reported($dupes, 18446744073709551615), "\n";
echo Ask::reported($empty, 1), "\n";
echo Ask::reported($dupes, 2), "\n";

// 6. `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R2's trailing bag sits in front of the report here as everywhere.
try {
    Test::assertCount($dupes, 2, {message: "the seed loaded two rows"});
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $withMessage) {
    echo $withMessage->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
onDiagonal=5/5 offDiagonal=25/25 agreed=30/30
held failed
held held held
failed
held failed
held
Core\Test::assertCount failed: `$actual` holds 3 entries, `$expected` is 18446744073709551615
Core\Test::assertCount failed: `$actual` holds 0 entries, `$expected` is 1
Core\Test::assertCount failed: `$actual` holds 3 entries, `$expected` is 2
the seed loaded two rows: Core\Test::assertCount failed: `$actual` holds 3 entries, `$expected` is 2
